Given a 2 × n board and tiles of size 2 × 1, find the total number of ways to completely fill the board using these tiles. Each tile can be placed either horizontally i.e., as a 1 x 2 tile or vertically i.e., as 2 x 1 tile. Two tiling arrangements are considered different if the placement of at least one tile differs.Examples: Input: n = 4 Output: 5Explanation: For a 2 x 4 board, there are 5 ways. Input: n = 3 Output: 3Explanation: For or a 2 x 3 board, there are 3 ways. Try It YourselfTable of Content[Naive Approach] Using Recursion - O(2^n) Time and O(n) Space[Better Approach 1] Using Top-Down DP (Memoization) - O(n) Time and O(n) Space[Better Approach 2] Using Bottom-Up DP (Tabulation) - O(n) Time and O(n) Space[Expected Approach] Using Space Optimized DP - O(n) Time and O(1) Space[Naive Approach] Using Recursion - O(2^n) Time and O(n) SpaceThe idea is to use Recursion and breaking the larger problem (size n) into smaller subproblems (sizes n-1 and n-2).At any point, we have two choices:Place one vertical tile (2 × 1): This tile will cover one column of the board, leaving a smaller board of size 2 × (n - 1) to be filled.Place two horizontal tiles (1 × 2): These two tiles will cover two columns together, leaving a smaller board of size 2 × (n - 2) to be filled.So, to find the total number of ways to fill a 2 × n board, we just need to sum the number of ways to fill the smaller boards obtained by these two choices.
